<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine VI</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/771">771</a>–c. 797</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine VI (771–c. 797) was Byzantine emperor from 780 to 797, at first under the regency of his mother Irene. He was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kardam">Khan Kardam</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> at Markellai in 792, and his divorce and remarriage alienated the church. In 797 Irene had him blinded and took the throne herself, becoming the first woman to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in her own name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-vi.jpg" alt="Constantine VI" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: АНО &quot;Международный нумизматический клуб · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:INC-3040-r_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%B4._%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BD_VI_%D0%B8_%D0%98%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0._793%E2%80%94979_%D0%B3%D0%B3._(%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%81).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constantine was born on 14 January 771, the only child of Emperor Leo IV and Irene. His father crowned him co-emperor in 776, and he succeeded as sole emperor in 780 at the age of nine, with his mother and her minister Staurakios as regents. In 788 Irene broke off his engagement to Rotrude, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the Armeniac troops rebelled against Irene, Constantine came to actual power in 790. After an unsuccessful campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> he restored his mother as co-ruler in 792, the year he was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kardam">Kardam</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> at the Battle of Marcellae. A plot then formed in favour of his uncle, the Caesar Nikephoros. Constantine had his uncle blinded and the tongues of his father's four other half-brothers cut out. He also blinded the Armeniac general Alexios Mosele, and the Armeniacs revolted, a rising he crushed with extreme cruelty in 793.</p><p>He then divorced his wife Maria of Amnia, who had borne no male heir, and married his mistress Theodote. The marriage was unpopular and canonically illegal and began the Moechian controversy. Patriarch Tarasios refused to officiate, and Theodote's uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> of Sakkoudion was imprisoned after breaking communion with him. The controversy cost Constantine most of his support in the church, which Irene took care to back in public.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 19 August 797 supporters of Irene captured Constantine, blinded and imprisoned him, and she was crowned as the first empress regnant. When he died is unknown, but it was before 805, and he may have died of his wounds soon after. He was buried in the Monastery of St Euphrosyne, which Irene had founded. In 800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperator-romanorum">Emperor of the Romans</a>, on the assertion that a woman could not rule in her own right, so Constantine was the last ruler recognised by the eastern empire, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the Western powers alike.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantine VI” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 8th century the emperor Constantine V waged repeated campaigns and won the Battle of Anchialus in 763, but Kardam defeated <b>Constantine VI</b> at Markellai in 792.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Supporters of the empress Irene seized her son, the Byzantine emperor <b>Constantine VI</b>, deposed him and had him blinded.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-19">19 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near the fortress of Markellai, close to present-day Karnobat, the Bulgarians under Khan Kardam routed the Byzantine army of Emperor <b>Constantine VI</b>, who fled the field.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=787" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constantine VI"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=787">What was here in 787 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
